<div class="specimen">
  <p class="specimen__note">
    <b>Body</b>Source Serif 4 on a 34rem measure. Wrap runs of prose in
    <code>.flow .measure</code>.
  </p>
  <div class="flow measure">
    <p>
      The web rewards work that stays simple for long enough to be maintained.
      Most of what follows is an argument for doing less at request time and
      more at build time, then letting the network do what it is good at.
    </p>
    <p>
      That means <strong>pre-rendering by default</strong>, reaching for
      <a href="#">progressive enhancement</a> before client-side rendering, and
      treating <em>every</em> runtime dependency as a cost to justify.
    </p>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="specimen">
  <p class="specimen__note"><b>Lists</b>ul, ol and dl.</p>
  <div class="flow measure">
    <ul>
      <li>Render ahead of the request</li>
      <li>Cache at the edge</li>
      <li>Hydrate only what needs it</li>
    </ul>
    <ol>
      <li>Write the content</li>
      <li>Build the pages</li>
      <li>Deploy the folder</li>
    </ol>
    <dl>
      <dt>Time to first byte</dt>
      <dd>Measured from the nearest edge location, not the origin.</dd>
      <dt>Build time</dt>
      <dd>Paid once per deploy rather than once per visitor.</dd>
    </dl>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="specimen">
  <p class="specimen__note"><b>blockquote</b>Pull quote with optional cite.</p>
  <div class="measure">
    <blockquote>
      The fastest page is the one that was already finished before anybody asked
      for it.
      <cite>Talk notes, Jamstack Conf</cite>
    </blockquote>
  </div>
</div>

